WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:06.970
As the hammer and sickle descended above the
Kremlin on Christmas Day, 1991, America breathed

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:09.750
a final sigh of relief.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:14.190
The days of duck-and-cover were officially
over.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:19.769
For the first time in modern history, one
nation prevailed in nearly every domain — from

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:23.520
military to economic, cultural, and scientific.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.400
And it wasn’t even close.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:32.279
In the absence of an “imminent threat”
— a worthy strategic rival motivating it

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:37.700
to put men on the moon, sprint faster, or
invent the internet — the United States

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:43.170
experienced a luxury without precedent: a
choice.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:49.350
So secure was its territory, so superior was
its Navy, and dominant, its economy, that

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:52.059
it could simply decide its future.

00:00:52.059 --> 00:00:59.140
Did it want to buy with this lead the ability
to ease its foot off the gas — to “right-size”

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:03.379
its military for the new, lower level of danger?

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:07.860
For a brief moment, that seemed like a real
possibility.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:13.049
Bush Senior spoke of a “Peace Dividend”
— the productive redistribution of resources

00:01:13.049 --> 00:01:15.150
away from the military.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:23.100
98 major U.S. military bases were closed between
1988 and 95, along with hundreds of smaller

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.460
installations, saving taxpayers an estimated
$57 billion.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:33.671
And the total defense budget decreased or
at least stayed flat, relative to GDP, for

00:01:33.671 --> 00:01:37.670
all but one of the next fifteen years.

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:43.950
By no means was America relinquishing its
dominance, but it was recalibrating.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:51.009
Then, on September 11th, 2001, the decision
was made.

00:01:51.009 --> 00:02:07.229
This time, definitively.

00:02:07.229 --> 00:02:12.450
Rather than accept the unthinkable — that
America, like any country, will always be

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:18.180
somewhat vulnerable, George Bush redefined
the word ‘safe’.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:23.300
Safety was no longer about making informed
risk-reward trade-offs.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:28.940
Since 9/11, ‘safety’ in America means
near invincibility — nothing less than zero

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:35.340
casualties with zero room for error — everywhere,
at all times.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.450
In military parlance, this meant shifting
from a “threat-based” model — in which

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:45.180
we plan and budget our forces based on those
of our enemies — to a “capabilities-based”

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:52.180
approach — in which we strive for the ability
to do everything, regardless of actual threats.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:56.750
It didn’t matter whether a hypothetical
attack was realistic, never mind actually

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:02.970
advantageous to the attacker, we should be
able to prevent it just in case!

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:08.060
Should two separate wars ever break out simultaneously,
the U.S. military should be able to fight

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.659
both — each with maximum force.

00:03:11.659 --> 00:03:17.700
Thus, after a brief 10-year intermission,
this new, unattainable definition of safety

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:23.920
allowed existing yet previously disregarded
threats like North Korea, Iran, and Iraq to

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.170
neatly fill the gap left by the Soviet Union.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:32.800
In fact, because they were numerically more
of them, to many people, the world felt even

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.160
more dangerous.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:40.629
Unlike the Soviets, who Americans feared for
their strength — real or imagined — these

00:03:40.629 --> 00:03:45.970
new threats were said to be irrational, and
thus undeterrable.

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:51.470
The United States decided that it could no
longer afford to take its foot off the gas.

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:56.739
Given how fast the enemy was approaching,
it had no choice but to go full steam ahead

00:03:56.739 --> 00:04:01.450
— using what lead it still had just to maintain
it.

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:05.459
Mere dominance alone spelled eventual defeat.

00:04:05.459 --> 00:04:12.879
Instead, Bush Junior endorsed a strategic
ideal that had long lingered in American politics:

00:04:12.879 --> 00:04:14.390
primacy.

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:19.960
Where dominance means having a stronger military
— beating everyone else at the game, primacy

00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:24.911
means being so far ahead that you set the
very rules of the game.

00:04:24.911 --> 00:04:29.350
Not only can you defeat anyone else in battle,
but you ensure that no one else even has the

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:32.770
will to fight in the first place.

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:37.790
Bush spelled this out clearly when he said,
quote, "If we wait for threats to fully materialize,

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:40.540
we will have waited too long”.

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:44.800
The U.S., he said, must maintain “strengths
beyond challenge”.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:50.760
And since there already were no challengers
nearby, this meant traversing an entire ocean

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.460
in either direction.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:58.340
The world is all too familiar with the fallout
from this redefinition of ‘safety’…

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:04.840
the war in Afghanistan, Yemen, Iraq, and long
lines at American airports, to name just a

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:05.840
few.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:10.630
But by far the most overlooked consequence
was Bush’s withdrawal of something called

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:13.600
the Anti-Ballistic Missile Treaty.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:21.460
And to understand why, we have to go way back
to the early Cold War…

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:26.710
For those of us born in the last roughly 50
years, the only world we know is one of Mutually

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:30.880
Assured Destruction — an obviously terrifying
concept.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:37.419
But before MAD there was an even darker 20-year
period most either don’t know or prefer

00:05:37.419 --> 00:05:38.930
not to think about.

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:46.180
…A time when the United States was seriously
talking about winning nuclear war.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:51.530
The Truman, Eisenhower, and Kennedy administrations
commanded significantly larger nuclear stockpiles

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:56.630
than the Soviets, giving them the theoretical
ability to knock out all their opponent’s

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:59.720
forces before it had a chance to respond.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:04.840
And because they suspected — correctly — that
this lead wouldn’t last, they felt the pressure

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:08.610
to use it before you lose it.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:14.380
From this perspective that war was inevitable,
a larger and preemptive first strike was actually

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:16.970
safer and more responsible.

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:24.090
Better to overwhelm the Soviets now than risk
a global nuclear apocalypse later on.

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:28.911
For years, not only were extensive battle
plans drafted for a pre-emptive attack, but

00:06:28.911 --> 00:06:32.280
many called for using everything at once.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:37.770
Later, when the Americans conjured fever dreams
of a surprise attack from the Soviets, they

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:42.280
concluded that although one-third of the U.S.
population would be killed, victory would

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:48.990
be America’s, as it would then be able to,
quote, “eliminate the USSR as a world power”.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:54.090
To the U.S. military at the time, nukes were
just a bigger, faster, stronger version of

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:57.050
the bombs they already had.

00:06:57.050 --> 00:07:02.970
As if all this weren’t insane enough, civil
applications were suggested for nukes.

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:08.760
Edward Teller, a scientist close to Ronald
Reagan, proposed such bizarre plans as using

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:14.930
nuclear weapons to widen the Panama Canal
and speed-up industrial mining.

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:18.910
This was the Wild West for crazy atomic ideas.

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:26.020
Fortunately, sanity ultimately prevailed — or,
more accurately, the Soviets started catching

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:32.160
up — and nuclear war began to be seen as
an unwinnable outcome.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:36.930
Faced with the possibility that one side might
suddenly ‘win’ the arms race — by building

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:42.310
either enough weapons to overwhelm the other,
or an effective defense against them — and

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:49.540
feel the need, as America nearly did, to use
this advantage — they came to an agreement.

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:55.870
The 1972 Anti-Ballistic Missile Treaty limited
the defenses each country could build — forcing

00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:59.340
them to confront their vulnerability.

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:04.009
In the end, the treaty didn’t stop the Soviets
from building more weapons, but it did give

00:08:04.009 --> 00:08:07.280
both nations an interest in peace.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:11.500
And that peace would last for the remainder
of the Cold War.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:18.069
The treaty even survived the fall of the Soviet
Union, with Russia inheriting its place.

00:08:18.069 --> 00:08:22.620
That is, until Bush withdrew after 9/11.

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:27.069
Leaving the treaty, the President explained,
would allow the country to erect a shield

00:08:27.069 --> 00:08:32.719
protecting all 50 states from nuclear missiles
by intercepting them on the way.

00:08:32.719 --> 00:08:34.849
The only problem was that…

00:08:34.849 --> 00:08:37.300
it didn’t exist.

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:43.640
In fact, (spoilers), 20 years and many billions
of dollars later, and America is still not

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:45.320
protected.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:51.529
In other words, this was Bush announcing America’s
intention to become untouchable.

00:08:51.529 --> 00:08:56.390
This was him giving the world a heads up that
he intended to spend near unlimited funds

00:08:56.390 --> 00:09:02.490
to ensure the U.S. had no skin in the game,
allowing it to intervene when and where it

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:06.210
pleased, with no repercussions.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:09.760
Needless to say, this did not go well.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:15.800
Shockingly, Russia and China and North Korea
didn’t just shrug their shoulders and admit

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:16.800
defeat.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:22.410
Instead, they responded, predictably, with
countermeasures.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:27.280
Because military technology takes years to
develop, it took nearly two decades after

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:33.320
Bush’s withdrawal, but we are, right now,
entering an arms race.

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:37.890
America is racing toward invulnerability,
and its adversaries are inventing all kinds

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:42.240
of new and wacky ways to get through its defenses.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:48.550
There are a million such examples, but let’s
just start with the absolute simplest.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:56.120
American missile defenses have failed 44%
of their 18 tests between 1999 and 2018, despite

00:09:56.120 --> 00:10:00.620
being, quote, “scripted for success”,
according to the Pentagon’s former director

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:02.310
of testing.

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:07.160
Knowing that his life is at stake, imagine
that Kim Jong-Un, in a crisis, decided to

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:12.490
throw the U.S. the most basic of curveballs
— a decoy.

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:16.390
Because intercontinental ballistic missiles
travel through space, where there’s no air

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:21.550
resistance, even radar-reflective mylar balloons
would be difficult to distinguish from the

00:10:21.550 --> 00:10:24.010
actual warhead.

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:28.720
What that means is that Kim Jong-Un could
likely foil our ‘advanced’ missile defenses

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:32.020
with the right shaped balloon.

00:10:32.020 --> 00:10:37.339
Another tactic involves packing a dozen or
so nukes on a single rocket, a relatively

00:10:37.339 --> 00:10:42.130
cost-effective way to overwhelm America’s
defenses.

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:46.190
On the more high-tech side, there are hypersonics.

00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:51.970
While a traditional ballistic missile travels
up and down on a predictable trajectory, hypersonic

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:57.329
missiles glide all over the place, making
them far, far harder to intercept.

00:10:57.329 --> 00:11:02.589
Both China and Russia have already developed
this technology and could even fire them over

00:11:02.589 --> 00:11:08.290
the South Pole, bypassing America’s defenses
in Alaska.

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:11.370
None of these countermeasures are technically
insurmountable.

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:18.310
Given the size of its budget, we can expect
the U.S. military to eventually overcome them.

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:22.829
The problem is that this is not an even playing
field.

00:11:22.829 --> 00:11:29.500
Shooting bullets is just easier than intercepting
those bullets with other bullets.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:34.399
By the time the U.S. fills a loophole, at
least one of a half dozen of its enemies is

00:11:34.399 --> 00:11:37.250
likely to have found a new one.

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:43.370
Russia, for instance, recently tested a nuclear-powered
cruise missile with virtually unlimited range

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:47.810
that spews radioactive exhaust everywhere
it goes.

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:54.660
In 2019, it purchased 30 giant underwater
drone torpedoes capable of delivering a radioactive

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:58.630
tsunami to an American port undetected.

00:11:58.630 --> 00:12:03.120
These weapons just get crazier and crazier.

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:09.899
Now, the Pentagon sees in these new Russian,
Chinese, and North Korean weapons evidence

00:12:09.899 --> 00:12:13.680
that the world is becoming objectively more
dangerous.

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:18.380
Proof that we need stronger defenses now more
than ever.

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:23.680
But because its task is to implement, not
decide U.S. strategy, the Department of Defense

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:25.500
has a blindspot.

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:31.389
What it doesn’t see is that these new weapons
are being developed in response to America’s

00:12:31.389 --> 00:12:35.140
post-9/11 pursuit of invulnerability.

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:40.410
That our missile defenses are too weak to
actually protect us yet strong enough to provoke

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:45.139
our adversaries, who spend pennies on the
dollar to defeat them.

00:12:45.139 --> 00:12:52.829
That, in trying to make America completely
safe, we’re actually making it less safe.

00:12:52.829 --> 00:12:58.210
Even if we did manage to intercept, say, an
incoming North Korean missile, our defending

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:03.180
missile would look nearly indistinguishable
from a nuclear attack to someone like China

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:05.180
or Russia.

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:11.900
If Russia saw what could be a nuclear warhead
headed its way, would it immediately retaliate?

00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:16.779
Probably not, but “probably” isn’t very
reassuring when we’re talking about nuclear

00:13:16.779 --> 00:13:18.750
war.

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:23.490
In fairness, it's no surprise the military
pursues military solutions.

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:25.740
That’s its duty.

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:29.600
The problem is that there are no checks and
balances.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:35.350
Experts have had serious doubts about missile
defense since at least the 1960s, at one point

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:40.020
even laughing off Soviet attempts to build
it as a waste of money.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:44.870
But when faced with a crisis — whether during
the Reagan administration in the 80s, or Bush

00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:49.519
in 2001 — presidents have found it politically
easier to promise something that can never

00:13:49.519 --> 00:13:53.470
be delivered than admit vulnerability.

00:13:53.470 --> 00:13:58.230
Rather than restrain the military, politicians
enable it.

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:04.800
Then, because their goal of total invincibility
is unattainable, the defenses they build inevitably

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:08.509
fail, presenting them with a new problem…

00:14:08.509 --> 00:14:13.720
To be honest, they think, and admit that the
U.S. is vulnerable would scare our allies

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:15.860
and embolden our enemies.

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:22.410
In truth, the Institute for Defense Analyses
argues that not even the Pentagon likely knows

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:25.420
the real effectiveness of our missile defenses.

00:14:25.420 --> 00:14:30.300
They’ve been tested less than once a year
on average and only under the most optimal

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:31.560
conditions.

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:37.440
Thus, they feel they have no choice but to
mislead the public.

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:43.089
Despite a 44% failure rate, the Navy told
Congress in 2016 that it had “high confidence

00:14:43.089 --> 00:14:48.089
in its success”, and Bush warned “You
fire, we’re going to shoot it down” during

00:14:48.089 --> 00:14:50.400
a 2004 speech.

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:53.899
Similar claims go as far back as Reagan.

00:14:53.899 --> 00:14:58.570
Americans, not knowing any better, believe
them.

00:14:58.570 --> 00:15:04.740
In one poll from 2000, for instance, 58% of
respondents assumed they were already protected

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:07.410
from nuclear missiles.

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:12.769
This overconfidence, in turn, leads the country
to pursue more aggressive tactics.

00:15:12.769 --> 00:15:18.350
It’s easy to dismiss Kim Jong-Un as an annoyance
when you believe you’re bulletproof.

00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:23.180
You might also feel less urgency to solve
the problem diplomatically.

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:30.380
Meanwhile, this dismissiveness makes Kim Jong-Un
feel the need to assert his earnestness.

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:35.380
Until and unless the U.S. takes him seriously,
he won’t be confident he’s deterring an

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:37.630
attack.

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:43.010
In other words, politicians, scared of being
‘weak’ in a crisis, enable the military.

00:15:43.010 --> 00:15:48.620
Then, trapped by their own decisions, both
mislead the public, who adopt a more aggressive

00:15:48.620 --> 00:15:49.880
posture.

00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:57.509
Finally, that aggressive posture leads to
more crises, completing the cycle.

00:15:57.509 --> 00:16:02.850
The point is not that America is to blame
for all of Russia or China or North Korea’s

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:05.519
actions — it isn’t.

00:16:05.519 --> 00:16:09.760
Neither does this have anything to do with
the war in Ukraine.

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:13.519
Nor is this an argument against all missile
defense.

00:16:13.519 --> 00:16:19.139
When and where it actually saves lives, that’s
a different story!

00:16:19.139 --> 00:16:24.870
The point is that America can effectively
defend itself and its allies, even while maintaining

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:29.690
its dominance, without pursuing policies that
only make it unsafe.

00:16:29.690 --> 00:16:37.089
There’s no trade-off here between protecting
American lives and not irritating evil dictators,

00:16:37.089 --> 00:16:44.220
because our missile defenses only make the
world more dangerous for Americans.

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:48.530
The United States is in a strange position.

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:53.639
Separated from all its adversaries by the
two largest oceans, blessed with the geography

00:16:53.639 --> 00:16:59.899
of success, and spoiled with unthinkable wealth
and influence, it’s the only country in

00:16:59.899 --> 00:17:03.940
the world where primacy feels like a choice.

00:17:03.940 --> 00:17:10.970
The only one where absolute, total, impenetrable
security feels genuinely achievable — if

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:16.890
only we fixed this or that technical problem
or spent x number of dollars more.

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:24.250
But the U.S. has spent no shortage of money
— $350 billion by some estimates — and

00:17:24.250 --> 00:17:29.050
no shortage of time — over seventy years.

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:34.490
Every modern President — Republican or Democrat
— has either endorsed, pursued, or at least

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:38.340
left untouched America’s missile defenses.

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:42.440
The United States hasn’t ‘failed’ for
lack of trying.

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:48.210
Its failed because the goal of total invincibility
is impossible.

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:52.789
The problem arises when this single-minded
obsession prevents America from noticing when

00:17:52.789 --> 00:17:59.070
it becomes self-destructive — when our desire
to become invincible puts us in needless additional

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:00.880
danger.

00:18:00.880 --> 00:18:06.770
To resist this very human temptation to seek
total security is unthinkable.

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:13.080
To admit vulnerability contradicts basic military
strategy and common sense.

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:17.720
And yet, the United States must.

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:23.240
Its refusal to do so is already responsible
for one of the largest unforced errors in

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:29.080
the history of U.S.-China relations and it
all has to do with nuclear weapons.

00:18:29.080 --> 00:18:34.120
In this new episode of my Nebula Original
series “China, Actually” — out now on

00:18:34.120 --> 00:18:39.049
today’s sponsor, Nebula — I explain why
China has one of the most sensible and level

00:18:39.049 --> 00:18:41.610
headed policies toward nuclear weapons.

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:45.220
And why that might be about to change.

00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:49.460
This is the sixth video in the series and
you can watch all of them, plus get access

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:55.890
to dozens of other exclusives, not to mention
